From: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 02:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: flowtable: clear IPS_OFFLOAD only on first teardown

flow_offload_teardown() clears IPS_OFFLOAD_BIT of the conntrack on
every call, while only the first call moves the flow to
NF_FLOW_TEARDOWN. A torn down flow with a hardware entry is not freed
by the GC pass that tears it down. It stays in the table until its
FLOW_CLS_DESTROY work has completed, at least one more pass, and every
pass in between calls flow_offload_teardown() again as long as the
flow is expired, its route is stale or its conntrack is dying.

In the meantime the next packet of the connection that takes the slow
path sets the bit again and adds a new flow for the same tuple. The
next repeated teardown of the old flow then clears the bit that the
new, live flow holds, and the next slow path packet adds a second live
flow for the tuple, as rhashtable_insert_fast() does not reject
duplicate keys. When that duplicate is torn down in turn, it clears
the bit of the live flow again, and the cycle repeats for as long as
packets of the connection reach the slow path.

With SOE packet offload on EN7581, a LAN host sent UDP datagrams into
an ESP tunnel that arrived as two IP fragments each. The flowtable
does not handle fragments, so every reassembled datagram reached the
forward chain. The conntrack of the stream got one new flow_offload
per GC tick, and each of them was destroyed again a few seconds later.
The PPE never bound these duplicates, as the entry for the tuple
belonged to the live flow, so their stats reported them idle and they
expired. Every destroy of an SOE flow also quiesced the IPsec path of
the PPE for all SAs.

Clear the bit only when the flow enters NF_FLOW_TEARDOWN, and set that
flag first, so that lookups already skip the flow by the time the slow
path can add the next one. The bit is taken with test_and_set_bit() by
the flow_offload expression, the FLOWOFFLOAD target and act_ct, which
either insert a flow or clear the bit again when they fail. A flow
enters NF_FLOW_TEARDOWN only in flow_offload_teardown() and is freed
only after that, by the GC or by nf_flow_table_free(). Each flow thus
releases the bit exactly once, while it still holds it: the bit is no
longer cleared under a live flow, and it cannot stay set without a
flow that will clear it. Later teardowns of the same flow return
early, as they already skipped flow_offload_fixup_ct().

The bit is still cleared before flow_offload_fixup_ct() updates the
timeout of the conntrack. nf_flow_table_extend_ct_timeout() reads that
timeout before it tests the bit, and extends a timeout below five
minutes to a day while the bit is set. The test_and_set_bit() that sat
between the clear and the update ordered the two, so add
smp_mb__after_atomic() in its place. Without it, a GC pass that checks
the flow while the packet path tears it down can see the shortened
timeout with the bit still set and extend it to a day again.

Signed-off-by: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
---
--- a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
@@ -422,9 +422,20 @@ static void flow_offload_del(struct nf_f
 
 void flow_offload_teardown(struct flow_offload *flow)
 {
+	/* A torn down flow can stay in the table for several GC passes, and
+	 * by then IPS_OFFLOAD_BIT may belong to a new flow of the conntrack.
+	 * Only the first teardown releases the bit that this flow holds.
+	 */
+	if (test_and_set_bit(NF_FLOW_TEARDOWN, &flow->flags))
+		return;
+
 	clear_bit(IPS_OFFLOAD_BIT, &flow->ct->status);
-	if (!test_and_set_bit(NF_FLOW_TEARDOWN, &flow->flags))
-		flow_offload_fixup_ct(flow);
+	/* nf_flow_table_extend_ct_timeout() reads ct->timeout before it tests
+	 * the bit. Order the clear before the timeout update below, so that
+	 * the GC does not extend the new timeout to a day.
+	 */
+	smp_mb__after_atomic();
+	flow_offload_fixup_ct(flow);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(flow_offload_teardown);
 
